=begin pod

=TITLE App::FontSample

=SUBTITLE Create PDF font samples from C<PDF::Content::FontObj> objects

=head1 DESCRIPTION

C<App::FontSample> can create four font sample types
(click each link to view on GitHub):

=item L<specimen   | ./examples/pdf/example-specimen.pdf>
=item L<comparison | ./examples/pdf/example-comparison.pdf>
=item L<collection | ./examples/pdf/example-collection.pdf>
=item L<characters | ./examples/pdf/example-characters.pdf>

C<App::FontSample> does not provide fonts other the built-in core fonts
which are limited to 256 glyphs.
Library callers can supply
one or more C<PDF::Content::FontObj> objects. The installed C<font-sample>
program can load an OTF (or TTF) file directly.

The current release supports Letter and A4 paper, portrait and landscape
orientation, configurable margins, JSON job files, registered language
pangrams, and the four layouts shown above.

=head1 INSTALLATION

Install C<App::FontSample> from the Raku ecosystem with:

=begin code :lang<text>
zef install App::FontSample
=end code


=head1 EXAMPLE USE

The subroutine exposed for public use is shown here:

=begin code
sub create-font-sample(
    PDF::Content::FontObj:D $font,
    Str:D :$name!,
    IO() :$output! where *.so,
    Str:D :$paper = 'Letter',
    Str :$media,
    Bool:D :$landscape = False,
    Numeric:D :$margin = 36,
    Str:D :$family = '',
    Str:D :$style  = '',
    Str:D :$layout = 'specimen',
    Str:D :$title  = 'Font Samples',
    Str :$language = 'en',
    :$debug,
    *%options,
    --> IO::Path:D
) is export {...}
=end code

The easiest first test uses the standard PDF core font Times-Roman, so no
external font file is required.

=begin code :lang<raku>
use PDF::API6;
use App::FontSample;

my $pdf = PDF::API6.new;
my $font = $pdf.core-font: :family<Times-Roman>;

create-font-sample(
    $font,
    :name<Times-Roman>,
    :title<Font Specimen>, # the default type is 'specimen'
    :language<en>,
    :output<sample.pdf>,
);
=end code

The B<specimen> identifies the font, labels the alphabet and
punctuation sizes, labels the pangram as C<English (en)>, and includes
a size waterfall with each point size shown at the left.

=head1 CURRENT OUTPUT LAYOUTS

=head2 specimen

The C<specimen> layout produces one specimen page per font. It shows the font
name, alphabet, numerals and punctuation, a labeled language pangram, and a
size waterfall.

See an example at L<click here | documents/example-specimen.pdf>.

An explicit pangram must also identify its language so the PDF can label it:

=head2 NOTE

The B<collection> and B<comparison> sections below render each font at
the same nominal point size. Visible character heights may differ
because each typeface has its own font metrics.

=head2 comparison

The B<comparison> layout displays the same text in several fonts at one
stated point size. The font name is shown above each sample. The layout
continues onto additional pages when needed.

=head2 collection

The B<collection> layout is a compact one-page inventory of fonts. Each font
name is followed by a short typographic identification string. The page title
states the sample point size.

If the requested collection cannot fit within the page margins, the layout
reports an error rather than overflowing the page.

=head2 characters

The B<characters> layout displays selected characters in a grid. The page
heading identifies the font and glyph point size, and each cell labels the
glyph with its Unicode code point.

Library callers may supply B<characters> as a string or as a positional
collection containing one-character strings, integer Unicode code points, or
strings such as C<U+0041>.

=head1 FONT COLLECTIONS

A comparison or collection uses B<App::FontSample::FontEntry> objects.

=begin code :lang<raku>
use App::FontSample;
use App::FontSample::FontEntry;

my @entries;

@entries.push: App::FontSample::FontEntry.new(
    :name<Times-Roman>,
    :font($times),
);

@entries.push: App::FontSample::FontEntry.new(
    :name<Helvetica>,
    :font($helvetica),
);

create-font-collection-sample(
    @entries,
    :layout<comparison>,
    :comparison-size(18),
    :output<comparison.pdf>,
);
=end code

=head1 PAPER AND PAGE OPTIONS

Both C<Letter> and C<A4> are supported. Pages may be portrait or landscape,
and the margin may be set in PostScript points (72 per inch)..

=head1 LANGUAGE PANGRAMS

The current built-in pangram registry includes English under the two-letter
code C<en>.

The resulting specimen labels the text C<English (en) pangram>.

List the currently registered language codes with:

=begin code :lang<text>
font-sample --languages
=end code

Applications may register additional pangrams and language names at runtime.

=head1 OTF AND TTF FILES

The installed C<font-sample> program loads an OTF or TTF file directly
and uses one of the same layout names as shown above.

=begin code :lang<text>
font-sample \
    --font=/path/to/font.otf \
    --output=sample.pdf \
    --layout=specimen \
    --language=en
=end code

=head1 JSON INPUT

C<font-sample> can read one or more jobs from a JSON definition file:

=begin code :lang<text>
font-sample --config=font-samples.json
=end code

For example:

=begin code :lang<json>
{
    "output": "output/sample.pdf",
    "layout": "specimen",
    "language": "en",
    "fonts": [
        {
            "file": "fonts/MyFont.otf",
            "name": "My Font"
        }
    ]
}
=end code

=head1 REPRODUCIBLE PDF OUTPUT

The normal PDF production process creates a unique document identification
number and other metadata that may be different. This program by default
does not do that so identical visual documents should compare
identifally byte for byte. There is an option to allow the normal process to
prevail.

The defaut PDF output is modified so normal unique document identification
and other metadata changes are turned off. As a result they should be
byte-for-byte reproducible.

use C<:reproducible> from Raku or C<--reproducible> in direct command-line mode.
JSON jobs may use C<"reproducible": true>. Normal PDF generation retains the
default unique document identification and metadata behavior.

=head1 OPTIONAL NOTOFONTS-OT USE

C<NotoFonts-OT> is not a dependency of C<App::FontSample>. It is one
optional source of C<PDF::Content::FontObj> objects.

=begin code :lang<raku>
use NotoFonts-OT;
use App::FontSample;
use App::FontSample::FontEntry;

my @entries;

for <
    NotoSerif-Regular
    NotoSerif-Bold
    NotoSans-Regular
    NotoSans-Bold
> -> $code {
    my $font = get-loaded-font($code);

    @entries.push: App::FontSample::FontEntry.new(
        :name($code),
        :$font,
    );
}

create-font-collection-sample(
    @entries,
    :layout<comparison>,
    :comparison-size(18),
    :output<noto-comparison.pdf>,
);
=end code

=head1 EXAMPLES

C<examples/current-capabilities.raku> uses PDF core fonts and creates four
PDFs, one for each current layout:

=item C<example-specimen.pdf>
=item C<example-comparison.pdf>
=item C<example-collection.pdf>
=item C<example-characters.pdf>

Run the examples like this:

=begin code
raku -Ilib examples/current-capabilities.raku
=end code

=head1 TESTING

Run the normal test suite with C<mi6 test> (for modules under
C<App::Mi6> management) or C<zef .>.

=begin code :lang<text>
mi6 test
=end code

The normal test suite does not require C<NotoFonts-OT>.

